---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import EvidenceGatewayFlow from '../../components/EvidenceGatewayFlow.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'evidence-gateway')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Let other services check information from your register."
  description="A procurement service may need to confirm a supplier’s registration without receiving its full record. Evidence Gateway checks your register and returns the agreed information with a digital signature. Your institution decides which services may ask and what they can receive."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="evidence-example-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">One example: a supplier registration check</p>
        <h2 id="evidence-example-title">Does this supplier have an active business registration?</h2>
        <p>A government procurement service needs to check a supplier’s registration before it processes an application. The business registration authority connects Evidence Gateway to its business register. The procurement service receives the registration status, without the address, filing history or other details.</p>
      </div>
      <EvidenceGatewayFlow />
      <p class="product-note">The digital signature lets the service verify that the expected issuer produced the answer and that the answer has not been altered. It does not prove that the source record is correct. The procurement service remains responsible for deciding what the answer means for the application.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="evidence-define-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practical capabilities</p>
        <h2 id="evidence-define-title">Define the question, its source, and who may ask.</h2>
        <p>The office that maintains the register agrees each answer with the services that will use it. Evidence Gateway applies that definition and the institution’s access rules whenever a connected service asks the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Return only the information needed</h3><p>Define the answer from approved source information. Return a person’s region of residence without a street address, or confirm that they are 18 or older without returning their date of birth.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Limit who may ask</h3><p>Authorize connected services for specific answers and purposes. Evidence Gateway checks that permission before reading the source and records the request outcome.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Check the answer’s dates</h3><p>The answer states when Evidence Gateway made the check and when the answer expires. Those dates do not show when the source record was last updated. Your implementation also checks how up to date the source information is.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="fit" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="evidence-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your organization</p>
        <h2 id="evidence-fit-title">Add information checks beside the register you already use.</h2>
        <p>Evidence Gateway can read an existing source. Your organization keeps its records in the current system, maintains their quality, and decides which connected services may receive each answer.</p>
        <p>Using Evidence Gateway does not require <a href="/products/base-registry/">Base Registry Engine</a>. If you choose both, your technical team configures the connection between them.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>Your institution defines each answer</dt><dd>Agree what the question and answer mean, which source provides the information, how recent that information must be, and which services may use it.</dd>
        <dt>Choose who delivers the implementation</dt><dd>Aubex, another delivery partner, or your own team can connect the source and receiving services, configure access and signing, and test the complete exchange. Agree who will host, operate and support the service.</dd>
        <dt>The receiving service owns its decision</dt><dd>Its team uses the answer alongside its own rules and other information. The answer does not decide eligibility or grant authority to change a record.</dd>
        <dt>Let people share an issued credential</dt><dd>Evidence Gateway can also issue a digitally signed statement, called a verifiable credential, that a person can keep in a digital wallet. The implementation connects and tests a compatible wallet and a service that accepts it. Issued credentials do not update with the register; Registry Stack currently has no way to cancel one before it expires. <a href="/solutions/verifiable-credentials/">Explore verifiable credentials</a>.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="evidence-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="evidence-developers-title">Request and verify your first signed answer.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial requests and verifies an adult-status answer without returning the person’s name or date of birth. The <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/products/registry-evidence/">product documentation</a> covers source connections and operation; the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/tutorials/return-a-governed-value/">defined-value tutorial</a> covers answers such as a residence region.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>{product.tutorialLabel} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>
